data.dns-servers#DNS Servers:
A server tag must be unique and follow the same rules as outbound tags: lowercase Latin letters, digits and _, starts with a letter, at most 24 characters.
Field Type Required Description tag string yes Unique identifier for this DNS server type string no DNS source type: static (default) or keenetic address string for static IP address of the DNS server, with optional port, for example "10.8.0.1", "10.8.0.1:5353", "2001:4860:4860::8888", or "[2001:4860:4860::8888]:5353" detour string no Outbound to use when contacting this DNS server The detour field is useful when a DNS server must be reached through a specific connection, usually the same VPN that will carry the matching traffic.
config.json{ "dns": { "servers": [ { "tag": "vpn_dns", "type": "static", "address": "10.8.0.1:5353", "detour": "vpn" }, { "tag": "google_dns", "address": "8.8.8.8" }, { "tag": "google_dns_v6", "address": "[2001:4860:4860::8888]:53" }, { "tag": "keenetic_dns", "type": "keenetic" } ] } }

data.ignore#\u003ccode\u003eignore\u003c/code:
Leaves traffic alone: keen-pbr does not touch it, and it follows the router’s normal routing as if keen-pbr were not there.
Use it for exceptions. Rules are checked from top to bottom and the first match wins, so put the ignore rule above a broader one. For example, the whole streaming list goes through the VPN except addresses from the streaming_direct list (direct is the ignore outbound from the example below):
config.json"rules": [ { "list": ["streaming_direct"], "outbound": "direct" }, { "list": ["streaming"], "outbound": "vpn" } ] Field Type Required Description tag string yes Unique identifier type string yes "ignore" config.json{ "outbounds": [ { "type": "ignore", "tag": "direct" } ] }

data.table#\u003ccode\u003etable\u003c/code\u:
Sends traffic through an existing routing table: usually 254, the system’s main table (the normal internet connection), or a table another service has created.
To see which tables exist and their names, run:
baship rule show cat /etc/iproute2/rt_tables Field Type Required Description tag string yes Unique identifier type string yes "table" table integer yes Existing routing table number config.json{ "outbounds": [ { "type": "table", "tag": "custom_table", "table": 200 } ] }

data.urltest#\u003ccode\u003eurltest\u003c/co:
Use this when you have several links (for example two VPNs and your normal internet) and want keen-pbr to pick a working one automatically. keen-pbr periodically opens url through each link and measures the response time.
If no link works, traffic for this outbound is blocked rather than leaking through your normal internet connection around the VPN.
Field Type Required Description tag string yes Unique identifier type string yes "urltest" url string yes URL used for availability and latency checks interval_ms integer no (default: 180000) Interval between checks in milliseconds probe_timeout_ms integer no (default: 5000) Timeout for one check in milliseconds tolerance_ms integer no (default: 100) Do not switch to another link if it is faster than the current one by less than this many milliseconds strategy string no (default: "priority") "priority": all traffic goes through the single fastest link. "balance": new connections are spread across the working links of the first working group in proportion to weight (not available on Keenetic) conntrack_on_switch string no (default: "preserve") What to do with open connections when moving to a faster link: "preserve" keeps them on the old link, "delete" deletes their conntrack entries so they reconnect. See Failover outbound_groups array yes Ordered list of link groups (see below) retry object no Check retries (see below) circuit_breaker object no Temporary exclusion of an unstable link (see below) config.json{ "outbounds": [ { "type": "urltest", "tag": "auto_select", "url": "https://www.gstatic.com/generate_204", "interval_ms": 180000, "probe_timeout_ms": 5000, "tolerance_ms": 100, "outbound_groups": [ { "members": [{ "outbound": "vpn1" }, { "outbound": "vpn2" }] }, { "members": [{ "outbound": "wan" }] } ], "retry": { "attempts": 3, "interval_ms": 1000 }, "circuit_breaker": { "failure_threshold": 5, "success_threshold": 2, "timeout_ms": 30000, "half_open_max_requests": 1 } } ] }

data.failover-or-balancing-for-all-internet-t:
To send all internet traffic through a urltest or icmptest outbound, add one default_gateway rule for IPv4 and one for IPv6 at the end of the rule list (nftables only). keen-pbr takes the LAN and directly connected networks from the main routing table and leaves them on normal routing.
By default these rules apply only to traffic from devices on your network. To include traffic from the router itself, set iproute.process_router_traffic to true.
config.json{ "iproute": { "process_router_traffic": true }, "route": { "rules": [ { "default_gateway": "ipv4", "outbound": "auto" }, { "default_gateway": "ipv6", "outbound": "auto" } ] } }
